  },
  {
    "file": "src/Kanban.Web/src/setupTests.ts",
    "line": 3,
    "summary": "Global ResizeObserver stub silences a browser API entirely for every test in the suite",
    "failure_scenario": "The no-op stub makes observe/unobserve/disconnect completely invisible. Any Fluent UI component that relies on ResizeObserver to size itself (e.g. overflow menus, responsive toolbars) will silently receive no resize notifications in every RTL test — rendering tests that exercise responsive layout meaningless. The right depth is to use a purpose-built package such as `@juggle/resize-observer` as a polyfill (preserving actual observer behaviour in jsdom) or, if the stub is genuinely needed only for a specific component, to scope it to that test file with `beforeEach`/`afterEach` rather than applying it globally. The global stub masks future layout regressions across the entire frontend test suite."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/Kanban.Api/Endpoints/InviteEndpoints.cs",
    "line": 27,
    "summary": "Endpoint manually constructs 422 Problem Details instead of delegating to DomainExceptionHandler, producing a structurally different error envelope",
    "failure_scenario": "DomainExceptionHandler (line 38-43) writes a ProblemDetails object whose extensions keys are `code` and `traceId`. The endpoint's manual 422 (lines 31-38) emits `code` and `errors` but omits `traceId`. When clients (frontend, API consumers) parse 422 responses they cannot assume a consistent shape: a validation failure has no `traceId` for correlation, while a BusinessRuleException does. If DomainExceptionHandler is ever extended — say, to add a `requestId` field or to change the casing of `code` — the manual path will not be updated, diverging further. The right approach is to throw a `BusinessRuleException` (or a new `ValidationException` subclass) from the validator result and let the handler produce the envelope, keeping one authoritative format for all 422s."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/Kanban.Business/Services/InvitationService.cs",
    "line": 88,
    "summary": "Refresh path manually constructs IssueInviteResponse inline instead of delegating to InvitationTransforms.ToResponse, duplicating the mapping logic",
    "failure_scenario": "InvitationTransforms.ToResponse (InvitationTransforms.cs lines 9-21) is the declared single source of truth for how an Invitation becomes an IssueInviteResponse. The refresh path (lines 88-93) bypasses it and hand-assembles the same three fields. When IssueInviteResponse gains a new required field (e.g. `IssuedToEmail` for display purposes), InvitationTransforms.ToResponse is updated, the new-invite path gets it automatically, but the refresh path silently produces a response missing the field — which can cause a runtime serialisation failure or a client-side null dereference. The fix is a one-line change: pass a synthetic Invitation (or add a `ToRefreshedResponse(string rawToken, DateTimeOffset expiresAt, string frontendBaseUrl)` overload to InvitationTransforms) and remove the inline construction."
  }
]
